faq:
  - q: "What is a hurdle rate, and why would bitcoin be one?"
    a: "A hurdle rate is the minimum return an investment must clear to be worth doing instead of the next-best use of the money. Corporate finance measures projects against the cost of capital; a project that returns less destroys value. For anyone who holds bitcoin or could, bitcoin&rsquo;s trend growth is a candidate hurdle: money put into something returning less than bitcoin&rsquo;s trend has, over that horizon, been the lower-returning choice &mdash; if the trend holds and the capital can wait through a drawdown. It is opportunity cost, not a monetary thesis."
  - q: "What is the opportunity cost of holding bitcoin &mdash; or of not holding it?"
    a: "Every capital decision is measured against its next-best alternative. If bitcoin&rsquo;s trend has grown faster than a given use of capital over the relevant horizon, then choosing that use instead of bitcoin has an opportunity cost equal to the gap &mdash; and choosing bitcoin over a use with a deadline or a liquidity need has the opposite cost. The tool computes the gap at a horizon you set; which side it falls on depends entirely on the horizon and on whether the capital can wait."
  - q: "Is bitcoin a better investment than paying off my mortgage?"
    a: "It depends on the horizon and on whether the money can wait. Paying down a 6% mortgage returns a guaranteed, after-tax 6%; bitcoin&rsquo;s trend return is higher over most horizons but is realised only by capital that can hold through a roughly &minus;73% drawdown without being forced to sell. Set your mortgage rate and horizon in the Personal lens above and the tool shows where the two cross. It is not advice &mdash; the mortgage is a certain return with no drawdown, which is worth something the arithmetic gap alone does not capture."
  - q: "Why do companies hold bitcoin instead of cash?"
    a: "A high hurdle explains it without any appeal to conviction. Cash held at money-market rates returns a few percent; if bitcoin&rsquo;s trend has grown faster over the horizon a treasury is managing, holding cash rather than bitcoin carries an opportunity cost, and behaviours like issuing debt against the stack or buying back stock follow from the same gap. The condition is that the business is cashflow positive &mdash; a company that may be forced to sell into a drawdown to meet obligations does not have capital that can wait, and the hurdle does not apply to it."
  - q: "Is bitcoin&rsquo;s hurdle rate a single number?"
    a: "No, and that is the central finding. The hurdle declines with the horizon of the decision, because the power law&rsquo;s growth rate decays with age. On a one-year-forward window bitcoin&rsquo;s trend growth is higher; on a thirty-year-forward window it is much lower. A three-year equipment purchase is measured against a different bar than a thirty-year infrastructure asset. Comparing every decision to one headline CAGR is wrong in a direction that matters &mdash; the tool matches the bar to your horizon."
